\section{Acquisition}{


The user is responsible for acquiring the two photographs
with matching entrance-pupil positions, aligned optical axes, and the tops
of both sensors oriented identically. These conditions are assumed by
the fit. The function does not detect, estimate, or compensate for
misalignment or other acquisition errors.

Corresponding points must be digitized in the same order. The split-view
feature in
\href{https://github.com/GastonMauroDiaz/rcaiman-projects}{rcaiman-projects}
facilitates this task. The fitted coefficients and residuals combine
experimental error with model approximation error. Experimental error can
also be absorbed into the coefficients, so residuals alone do not measure
calibration accuracy.

This method allows several imaging systems to share a calibrated reference.
See \code{\link[=calibrate_geometry]{calibrate_geometry()}} for direct calibration with the simple method.
}

\section{Reference directions}{


Prepare the reference geometry with \code{\link[rcaiman:set_internal_geometry]{rcaiman::set_internal_geometry()}}.
Convert its digitized coordinates to pixel indices with
\code{\link[rcaiman:y_down_from_y]{rcaiman::indices_from_coord()}} and obtain internal directions with
\code{\link[rcaiman:add_directions]{rcaiman::add_directions()}}. Supply the resulting table as \code{pts_cal}. The
reference can use any model supported by \code{set_internal_geometry()}. Reference
directions describe the selected pixel centers. Coordinates in \code{pts_uncal}
remain continuous, without rounding to pixel indices.
}

\section{Radial projection}{


With \code{model = "radial_projection"}, radius in pixels is fitted as a
polynomial of \code{off_axis} in radians, without an intercept. Choose the
definitive, integer-coordinate \code{center_of_vision} before fitting and use
the same center in the resulting \link{geometry_spec}. The function uses the
supplied center unchanged; it does not estimate or snap it.

All paired observations enter the fit with equal weight. Any exclusions
must be made explicitly in both tables, preserving their correspondence.
Missing directions are not silently removed. The returned \link[stats:lm]{stats::lm}
predicts pixel radius from \code{off_axis} in radians, matching the units of
\code{add_directions()}.

The fitted radius at \code{normalization_angle} defines \code{normalization_radius}.
Dividing the fitted coefficients by this radius gives \code{projection_coef}.
Changing the normalization preserves the fitted projection in pixels.
A reference outside the sampled angular range involves extrapolation.
Inspect the fit before constructing a \link{geometry_spec}, where monotonicity
over the chosen calculation interval is checked. The fit does not infer
\code{max_observed_off_axis_angle} or choose \code{max_off_axis_angle}.
}

\section{Anisotropic projection}{


With \code{model = "anisotropic_projection"}, the reference table supplies
\code{off_axis} and \code{internal_azimuth} in radians. The definitive integer center
is used by \code{\link[rcaiman:add_image_polar]{rcaiman::add_image_polar()}} to prepare the uncalibrated points.
Distances from this center are divided by the image semidiagonal and
transformed as \code{atan(relative_radius / atan_scale)}. Image-plane angle
starts at the right and increases clockwise; internal azimuth starts at
the top of the sensor and increases clockwise.

Three models are fitted without intercepts: a polynomial for \code{off_axis},
and first-harmonic regressions for \code{sin(internal_azimuth)} and
\code{cos(internal_azimuth)}. Internal azimuth is recovered with \code{atan2(sin, cos)}.
All observations have equal weight and their pairing is retained, as in
the radial fit. The equations and parameter convention are documented in
\code{\link[rcaiman:set_internal_geometry]{rcaiman::set_internal_geometry()}}.

Supply the returned \code{parameters} and unchanged \code{center_of_vision} to
\code{\link[=add_geometry_spec]{add_geometry_spec()}}. The image dimensions used there must match
\code{width_uncal} and \code{height_uncal}: they define the semidiagonal used during
fitting and evaluation. \code{atan_scale} is supplied by the user, not fitted.
This model does not use \code{normalization_angle}.
}

